{"id":"7d7cf8e4-f132-461e-a405-57bfc86dca64","kind":"note","ref":{"span":"53584ced2798d839","key":"bablr1","file":"packages/editor/extensions/bridge/capability-stops.ts","baseline":{"blob":"0480612759e94c8888cce063950c544a04f67bb8","start":0,"end":91},"shape":{"type":"ImportDeclaration","atoms":["import","type","{","AskEvent",",","CapabilityChoice",",","DecideRequest","}","from","\"","@brianjenkins94/editor-api","\""]},"context":{"after":"78452bd3c2d1ad1e"}},"payload":{"text":"Capability stops, whichever debugger's (the run contract, @brianjenkins94/editor-api): a run stopped at a gated call\nasks on its line (`ask`), and the answer — `editor.capability.decide` (ARCHITECTURE.md, *Extension points* 3): a test\nrun's approval, runs_tests' — goes back to the debugger as a `decide` request.\n\nWhat an answer means is the editor's, not the debugger's: Allow, Skip or Deny for this call, this run (every call of\nits capability, until the run ends — a rule kept here, never written) or always (my policy override, as the preview's\nprompt writes it); a rule made in the margin's rule editor (saved in my policy, deciding the call as it does); or a\nresult given in the call's place. The debugger is told the verdict, and — when the answer changed it — the policy in\neffect from now on. Every debugger is given the policy as it launches (`__policy`, debug-events.ts).","wrote":["Brian Jenkins"]},"author":"local","createdAt":"2026-11-22T17:12:27.004Z","updatedAt":"2026-11-22T17:12:27.004Z"}
